The decision is straightforward once you identify the ordering requirement.

Use **HashMap** as the default. It has the best average-case performance, the lowest memory overhead, and works for the vast majority of key-value storage needs. Configuration maps, frequency counters, lookup tables — HashMap is the right choice when you don't care about iteration order.

Use **TreeMap** when you need keys in sorted order or need range operations. A dashboard showing metrics by timestamp, a dictionary with alphabetical traversal, or any scenario where `subMap(fromKey, toKey)` is valuable. The O(log n) cost is acceptable because the sorted invariant is the whole point.

Use **LinkedHashMap** when iteration order must match insertion order — building ordered JSON output, maintaining a predictable configuration display, or implementing an **LRU cache**. The LRU pattern is the interview-differentiator: construct with `new LinkedHashMap<>(capacity, 0.75f, true)` to enable access-order mode, then override `removeEldestEntry()` to evict when size exceeds the limit.

```java
Map<String, Object> lruCache = new LinkedHashMap<>(100, 0.75f, true) {
    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry eldest) {
        return size() > 100;
    }
};
```

This moves recently accessed entries to the tail. When a new entry pushes past 100, the least-recently-used entry (the head) is automatically removed.

When you call `map.put(key, value)`, the first step is computing the bucket index. Java doesn't use `key.hashCode()` directly — it applies a **spread function** that XOR-mixes the upper 16 bits into the lower 16.

```java
static final int hash(Object key) {
    int h;
    return (key == null) ? 0 : (h = key.hashCode()) ^ (h >>> 16);
}
```

The spread function exists because the bucket index is computed as `(capacity - 1) & hash`. With a small table (e.g., 16 buckets), only the **lowest 4 bits** of the hash determine the slot. If a class's `hashCode()` has poor distribution in the low bits — which is common — many keys cluster into the same buckets. The XOR spread ensures both halves of the hash contribute to the index.

Capacity is always a power of two, making `(capacity - 1)` a perfect bitmask. This is equivalent to `hash % capacity` but avoids integer division. Passing a non-power-of-two to the HashMap constructor rounds up internally.

Understanding the `modCount` mechanism explains exactly when the exception occurs and how to avoid it.

Every structural modification to an ArrayList or HashMap increments the internal `modCount` field. When you create an iterator, it copies this value as `expectedModCount`. On each `next()` call, the iterator checks `modCount == expectedModCount`. If they differ — exception.

The safe way to remove during iteration is using the **iterator's `remove()` method**, which updates both `modCount` and `expectedModCount` in sync.

```java
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next().startsWith("X")) {
        it.remove(); // safe — updates expectedModCount
    }
}
```

Java 8+ provides `removeIf()` which is cleaner: `list.removeIf(s -> s.startsWith("X"))`. Internally, it uses the same coordinated modification strategy.

For ConcurrentHashMap, you can safely modify during iteration because its iterators are weakly consistent. `map.forEach((k, v) -> { if (condition) map.remove(k); })` works without exception — though the removal may or may not be visible to the ongoing iteration depending on timing.

ListIterator maintains an implicit **cursor position** between elements. Understanding this cursor is essential for correct usage.

Imagine a list `[A, B, C]`. The cursor starts before A (position 0). `next()` returns A and moves the cursor between A and B. `previous()` would return A and move back before A.

`add(X)` inserts X **before the cursor**. If the cursor is between A and B after calling `next()`, `add(X)` produces `[A, X, B, C]` with the cursor now between X and B. Subsequent `next()` returns B — the inserted element is behind the cursor.

`set(Y)` replaces the last element returned by `next()` or `previous()`. If `next()` returned A, `set(Y)` changes the list to `[Y, B, C]`. Calling `set()` without a preceding `next()` or `previous()` (or after `add()`/`remove()`) throws `IllegalStateException`.

```java
ListIterator<String> li = list.listIterator();
while (li.hasNext()) {
    String s = li.next();
    if (s.equals("old")) li.set("new"); // replace in-place
    if (s.equals("target")) li.add("inserted"); // insert after target
}
```

`nextIndex()` and `previousIndex()` reveal the cursor position without advancing it — useful for debugging or conditional logic based on position.

The rule is simple: **if your key or element type is an enum, use EnumMap or EnumSet**. There is no trade-off — they are faster, use less memory, and iterate in enum declaration order.

EnumMap maintains **declaration order** during iteration — the order enum constants appear in the source code. This is deterministic and does not depend on hash codes, making test assertions and logging predictable.

```java
EnumMap<Status, List<Order>> byStatus = new EnumMap<>(Status.class);
for (Status s : Status.values()) byStatus.put(s, new ArrayList<>());
orders.forEach(o -> byStatus.get(o.getStatus()).add(o));
```

EnumSet has two internal implementations chosen automatically by `EnumSet.noneOf()`. **RegularEnumSet** uses a single `long` for enums with 64 or fewer constants — every operation is a single CPU instruction. **JumboEnumSet** uses a `long[]` for larger enums, still vastly more compact than HashSet.

One gotcha: EnumSet is abstract — you create instances via factory methods (`noneOf`, `of`, `allOf`, `copyOf`), not constructors. This is because the JVM picks the optimal implementation based on the enum's constant count.
